A||||8||Standard||Peeling clear coat||||5||
Z||000000||Standard||03-30-2000||04:26 AM||||What's the best way to get rid of peeling clear coat, other than getting a new paint job?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  There are quite a few places on my GT where the c/c is cracking and turning white as a result of sitting too long.  <P>I've tried rubbing compound, and it works to a certain extent, but my arm sure was sore the next day.  don't have the money to go out and buy a buffer or something like that. <P>How about wet-sanding?  what is the best/easiest/most efficient way to do it?  What grit sandpaper should I use?  How far down should I sand?  tell me anything and everything.<P>Thanks in advance!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||03-30-2000||06:11 PM||||You might be screwed...<P>But what I would try is sanding (wet) the area with some 1000 grit paper (lightly), follow up with 1500 and then 2000. Try to remove (carefully) as much of the damaged clear as you can without sanding down to the bare panel.<P>And I'm going to suggest this even though I've never tried it...get some clear in a spray can and then go over the area in question...let it dry a couple of days and then carefully wet sand both the repaired area and the area bordering the repair (use the 1500 and 2000 grit paper). Then go over with compound to remove the sanding marks.<P>It's either gonna look okay or be an eyesore...best possible solution is to repaint the panel(s) in question.<P>If you try what I've suggested, let us know how it works. And if it doesn't work...||||reg||9
Z||000002||lowCG||03-30-2000||11:18 PM||||Depending on how bad it is,that might work.<BR>I have two California cars,which have had a clearcoat painted over the oxidized paint in the past; I'll be sanding,one panel at a time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||03-31-2000||01:21 AM||||It's already and eyesore  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  just looking for anything that might possibly make it look a little better, until I have the money for a new paint job.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||03-31-2000||01:57 AM||||Well owning a paint shop or rather I have a paint shop in the Fiero Shop we tried to wet sand and we have buffers and special air sanders for polishing paint and wwe have not been succesfull in polishing a car that has the clear paint peeling. We have had to repaint in order to make it good.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Uaana||03-31-2000||02:57 AM||||Best of luck, S. I tried to get out some<BR>ox'd paint last summer by using some heavy and medium mguires liquid cleaners, managed to rub past what was left of the paint down to the obviously cracked primer, now trying my best to just do light touch up work so it is'nt an eye sore, good thing I have a night job and noone gets a close look during the day.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000006||lowCG||03-31-2000||09:24 AM||||I have a (black)California car with peeling clear,and my conclusion is to start sanding,one panel at a time.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||03-31-2000||04:36 PM||||<BR>Ogre posted a couple months ago about repairing the clear coat on black cars. Among other things, one of the tips was using black shoe polish! I tried to find the thread but the <I>Search</I> function wasn't working. Maybe someone else will have better luck and can post the link.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||03-31-2000||09:45 PM||||Yup. Kiwi Black Shoe Polish. It's by no means perfect but it's better than the the flaked clear coat. This looks bad on a colored paint. It is hidious on black. I had to do something. It was embarasing to drive it like that. I just don't have a wad of cash to paint it.<P>I took 400 grit ALOX papar and gently knocked off the worst off it. I used finer grits to get the lighter stuff. You have to be very gentle or you easily get thru the base. On a deck lid which is made from black plastic you can hide it ok. On doors and other panels were the substrate is light color your dead.<P>a useable wax was hard to find. all the regular car stuff get in the scratches and fills in white. Shoe polish is basically carnuba and lamp black as I remember. so I tried it and it worked pretty good. Far better than you'd expect. You could likely do this on other cars if you had wax of a near color. BTW.... If you do use the shoe polish, do it on a hot day it works best.<P>The keys to this working are carefull choice of paper grits, and gentle use of them. It won't ever be as good as a paint job, but it won't look like a rolling snow bank in july.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-31-2000).]||||reg||1
